\subsection{11. 🔬 Mixed Content}

\subsubsection{📈 Research Summary}

This section demonstrates a realistic mix of formatting elements as they would appear in an academic or technical document.

The study involved \textbf{three experimental conditions} 🧪:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textit{Baseline} — standard configuration with no modifications
  \item \textit{Treatment A} — applied the \texttt{--fast} flag with \texttt{batch_size=64} ⚡
  \item \textit{Treatment B} — applied the \texttt{--fast} flag with \texttt{batch_size=128} 🚀
\end{enumerate}

Results are summarized below:

| Condition   | Accuracy | Loss   | Duration |
|-------------|----------|--------|----------|
| Baseline    | 87.3\%    | 0.412  | 42 min ⏱️ |
| Treatment A | 91.1\%    | 0.301  | 28 min ⚡ |
| Treatment B | \textbf{93.4%}| \textbf{0.247} | 31 min 🏆 |

\begin{quote}
🏅 Treatment B achieved the best accuracy with only a marginal increase in training time compared to Treatment A.
\end{quote}

Key findings from the \texttt{results.json} output file:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=json]
{
  "best_condition": "treatment_b",
  "accuracy": 0.934,
  "loss": 0.247,
  "epochs": 50
}
\end{lstlisting}

Follow-up steps 📋:

\begin{itemize}
  \item ✅ Validate results on the held-out test set
  \item 🔍 Run \textbf{ablation study} to isolate the effect of \texttt{batch_size}
  \item 📝 Document findings in the final \textit{project report}
\end{itemize}

